The rear camera's five modes, each with its own link, and the crop rectangle

The driver had ONE mode, 2312x1304, because that was the one needed to get the
first image out. The phone's own vendor file carries FIVE streamConfiguration
and fourteen regSetting, that is, five complete modes.

    9248x6944   the 64 Mpx, unbinned
    4624x3472   2x2 binned
    3840x2160   2x2 binned, cropped to 16:9
    2312x1304   4x4 binned          <- the one that was already there
    1920x1080   4x4 binned, cropped to 16:9

The five tables use THE SAME 74 addresses and only 53 values change, so they go
exactly as they come out of the file, without touching a comma.

HOW I KNOW THE EXTRACTOR DOES NOT LIE

camara/sacar-los-modos.py validates itself by two paths:

  - In the five modes 0x0112/3 = 0x0a0a (RAW10) and 0x0114 = 0x02 (three trios)
    have to come out. If the address-value pairing were shifted, garbage would
    come out. It comes out right in all five.
  - The 2312x1304 table it generates is IDENTICAL, byte for byte, to the one
    that was already in the driver and really captures. That is the best check
    there is, because it compares against something that works.

The old script paired each regSetting against ALL the registerData in the file,
and that only gets the first one right. Now each regSetting is bounded with the
index of the next one.

ONE LINK FREQUENCY PER MODE

Each mode carries its own output PLL multiplier, so they emit at different
rates: x237 -> 379,2 MHz, x272 -> 435,2, x298 -> 476,8 and x325 -> 520. Before
there was a single constant. Now it is a menu of four, the mode picks its index,
and the device tree lists them all. The pixel rate stops being constant and is
computed from each mode's link.

The exposure, hblank and vblank limits are recomputed on a mode change with
__v4l2_ctrl_modify_range. Moving only the value and leaving the previous mode's
maximum makes libcamera's automatic control ask for things the sensor does not
support.

THE CROP RECTANGLE

get_selection did not exist, and libcamera warned:

    'imx682 12-001a': Unable to get rectangle 0 on pad 0/0
    The analogue crop rectangle has been defaulted to the active area size

Assuming that the area read equals the output size is false as soon as there is
binning: 2312x1304 comes out of a 9248x5216 window read four by four. From that
rectangle libcamera derives the field of view, which is what is needed for
digital zoom. The windows come from the vendor file itself (0x0346 and 0x034a)
and they match the binning in all five modes.

ORDER MATTERS

2312x1304 stays FIRST, which is the one used by default. Development runs on the
CPU and the cost grows with the pixels: on the front camera, 20 Mpx cost 53 ms
per frame against 20 ms at 5 Mpx. The big modes are available, but they are not
picked on their own.

A MISTAKE OF MINE ALONG THE WAY, which left the sensor not starting

I set the PIXEL_RATE range from mode 0 to mode 4 taking for granted that the
last one would be the fastest. It is the other way round: the 64 Mpx mode is the
one that emits the SLOWEST (PLL x237), so the minimum came out greater than the
maximum and the driver did not even register:

    imx682 12-001a: IMX682 detectado (0x0682)
    imx682 12-001a: fallo al crear los controles: -34
    imx682 12-001a: probe with driver imx682 failed with error -34

Now the range goes from the slowest link frequency to the fastest, which is what
it means, and not from the first mode to the last.


